<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Should I start leftover steroids at home if I can’t get an appointment today?</h3>
<p>It’s best not to self-start or use someone else’s medication. Steroid type, dose, timing, and your health conditions all matter. Call an ENT or urgent care and explain you have sudden hearing loss—clinics often prioritize these visits the same day. If you’re truly unable to be seen promptly, discuss next steps with a clinician by phone or telemedicine before taking anything.</p>

<h3>Is it ever too late to treat sudden hearing loss?</h3>
<p>Improvement can still happen beyond 72 hours, and many clinicians treat within a two-week window. Even if more time has passed, get evaluated—your team can discuss potential benefits vs. risks of therapy and help you plan rehabilitation options to minimize impact on daily life.</p>

<h3>Will my hearing fully recover?</h3>
<p>Many people improve, some fully, others partially. Factors can include how quickly treatment starts, the degree and pattern of loss, and your overall health. Follow-up audiograms help track change and guide next steps. If some loss remains, modern hearing technology and tinnitus therapies can dramatically reduce day-to-day difficulty.</p>

<h3>Could stress or allergies cause this?</h3>
<p>Stress and allergies can affect how ears feel, but sudden, significant hearing change deserves a medical exam and hearing test right away to rule out sensorineural loss. Don’t assume it’s “just allergies.” Quick evaluation keeps options open.</p>


